from register_common import add

add(99,'Smooth quadratic forms and their polar forms',[174,175],6861,6934,'proof sketched','B',
'For a positive-rank locally free module, a quadratic form is smooth when it is fiberwise nonzero and its projective quadric is smooth. A nondegenerate polar form implies smoothness; the converse holds in even rank or when2 is invertible. In odd rank characteristic2, smoothness instead means polar radical dimension1 and nonzero quadratic restriction to that line.',
'SGA7II XII1.1–1.2, printed62–64, gives exactly this distinction and an étale normal form. Conrad2014 AppendixC.1 gives the corresponding arbitrary-base treatment. The dimension of the projective quadric is rank−2, not rank−1 as printed in16.3.',
'SGA7II,Conrad2014','Proof: over a field a projective singular point is a nonzero vector v with B(v,−)=0 and Q(v)=0. If2 is invertible, B(v,v)=2Q(v) makes any radical vector singular. In characteristic2, an alternating polar matrix has even rank; smoothness permits exactly a one-dimensional radical on which Q is nonzero. These fiber criteria give the statement over a base.',group='quadratic')
add(100,'The universal corrected discriminant',[176,177],6940,7041,'proof sketched','B',
'For odd rank, the determinant of the universal polar matrix is divisible over Z by2 with content exactly2. Dividing that universal polynomial by2 defines the corrected or half-discriminant even on bases with2-torsion. It transforms by(det g)² and is a section of(det E)². For even rank use the full polar determinant.',
'Conrad2014 C.1.4, printed304–305, proves the universal polynomial, transformation law and smoothness criterion and credits independent discovery by Grothendieck and Kneser. Dolgachev–Duncan2018 Remark2.4 explicitly cites Grothendieck’s unpublished Appendix17.16. The draft’s formula2 dtilde²=d is a reading alarm; the universal identity is2 dtilde=d.',
'Conrad2014,DolgachevDuncan2018','computations.py ternary_half_discriminant, ternary_polar_determinant and standard_forms check the universal ternary formula and ranks1–8. Proof: modulo2 an odd alternating matrix has determinant0, so every universal coefficient is even. On the standard odd form the determinant is±2, so the content is exactly2. Prove the transformation identity over the torsion-free universal coefficient ring, divide by2 there, then specialize; this avoids illegal cancellation on a2-torsion base.',group='halfdisc')
add(101,'The odd-plus-odd direct-sum factor',[177],6984,7041,'false as stated','B',
'The direct-sum formula assigns factor2 when both summands have odd rank. With the manuscript’s unnormalized corrected discriminant det(B)/2 in odd rank, the required factor is4.',
'The determinant is multiplicative for a block-diagonal polar matrix. Thus two odd ranks each contribute a factor2 before their even-rank sum is formed. Conrad2014 uses compatible half-discriminant conventions; any sign-normalized variant must be declared separately.',
'Conrad2014','computations.py two_odd_ranks_sum computes Q=ax² and Q′=by²: corrected discriminants a,b; full determinant of their sum is4ab, not2ab. This counterexample is over Z[a,b] and remains valid on its nondegenerate open.',
refine='For the unnormalized convention δ_n=det(B) if n even and det(B)/2 if n odd, δ_(n+m)(Q⊥Q′)=4δ_n(Q)δ_m(Q′) when n,m are both odd, and equals their product otherwise.',group='halfdisc')
add(102,'Smoothness, invertible half-discriminant and fppf normal form',[178,179,180],7047,7162,'proof sketched','B',
'For positive rank, the quadratic form is smooth iff its corrected discriminant is invertible iff it becomes the standard split form after an fppf cover. Its isomorphism scheme with the standard form is affine and finitely presented; the orbit map is faithfully flat.',
'Conrad2014 C.1.4 and C.2.1, printed304–305 and309, prove these statements. SGA7II XII1.2 gives the étale normal form with a final unit coefficient in odd rank; adjoining its square root gives the fppf standard form. Rank0 is excluded by the draft’s fiberwise nonzero definition.',
'Conrad2014,SGA7II','Targeted proof check: SGA7II XII1.2 splits a hyperbolic plane using an étale isotropic section and inducts. In odd rank it leaves u z² with u invertible. A[t]/(t²−u) is finite locally free of rank2 and faithfully flat; after this cover scaling z gives the standard form. The isomorphism functor is cut out by polynomial equations in GL_n.',group='quadratic_forms')
add(103,'Quadratic forms as orthogonal torsors',[180,181],7128,7203,'proof sketched','B',
'Smooth rank-n quadratic forms are equivalent to fppf torsors under the standard orthogonal group O_n. The equivalence sends a form to its isomorphism torsor and a torsor to the descended standard vector bundle and quadratic form.',
'Conrad2014 AppendixC gives the group scheme and local-isomorphism facts; ordinary faithfully flat descent gives the equivalence. The torsor topology is fppf in all ranks, and cannot uniformly be weakened to étale in odd rank characteristic2.',
'Conrad2014,SGA7II','Proof: the isomorphism sheaf is locally nonempty by C-SA23-hyp-102 and O_n acts simply transitively on it. Conversely, descent of the finite locally free standard module and its quadratic tensor along a torsor yields a form. The two constructions are inverse with their morphisms.',group='quadratic_forms')
add(104,'Smoothness of the orthogonal group and the characteristic-two quotient',[181,182,183,184],7167,7304,'proof sketched','B',
'O(Q) is smooth when the rank is even or2 is invertible, and fails to be smooth in odd rank characteristic2. In odd rank the smooth special orthogonal factor gives O/SO=μ2. This quotient statement is not the even-rank characteristic2 component statement, which uses the Dickson invariant and Z/2.',
'Conrad2014 C.1.5, printed305–308, proves the smoothness criterion and explains O_(2m+1)=μ2×SO′ and the even-rank component distinction. The draft’s reduction through a μ2 torsor is valid in its odd-rank paragraph, not as an all-rank assertion.',
'Conrad2014','Targeted proof check: Conrad’s tangent calculation shows the extra infinitesimal dimension in odd rank characteristic2 and smoothness of the determinant1 factor there. The rank1 example O(a x²)=μ2 already proves nonsmoothness in characteristic2; its coordinate ring k[t]/((t−1)²) is nonreduced.',group='orthogonal')
add(105,'Étale versus finite flat trivialization',[181,182,183,184],7167,7304,'proof sketched','B',
'The draft asserts étale local triviality in the smooth-orthogonal cases and a Zariski-local finite étale strengthening via SGA3 XXIV; in odd rank it proposes first a finite locally free μ2 cover and then the smooth-group theorem. It supplies the rank-one characteristic2 example obstructing étale trivialization. These paragraphs assert cover results; the nearby priority and terminology question concerns the corrected discriminant and is separately recorded as C-SA23-hyp-106.',
'SGA3 XXIV4.2.3 proves that a torsor under a split reductive group over a semilocal base is trivialized by a finite étale surjective cover. Apply this to the split special orthogonal factor after the finite component torsor; in odd rank first use the finite locally free μ2 torsor. Finite presentation spreads the resulting cover and isometry from a local base to a Zariski neighborhood. Thus the asserted Zariski-local finite étale trivialization in the smooth cases, and finite flat trivialization in general, are established. This is not a theorem about arbitrary smooth groups.',
'Conrad2014,SGA7II,SGA3XXIV','Targeted proof check: original and corrected SGA3 XXIV4.1.1 and4.2.3 were read, including the split-torus/Pic(semilocal)=0 argument. The orthogonal component quotient is finite étale in even rank and when2 is invertible; the connected split SO factor is reductive. In odd rank O=μ2×SO′, so first take the finite locally free μ2 torsor and then the finite étale SO′ cover of the resulting semilocal ring. For a∈k* nonsquare in characteristic2, an isometry aT²≅T² needs a purely inseparable square root, which no separable extension contains; k[t]/(t²−a) is nevertheless a finite faithfully flat cover.',group='finite_trivialization')
add(106,'Historical priority and terminology for the half-discriminant',[181,182],7167,7242,'open on the pages','B',
'The author asks whether the corrected discriminant and the smoothness criterion are already known, and asks for their terminology and attribution.',
'Conrad2014 explicitly credits independent discovery by Grothendieck and M.Kneser. Dolgachev–Duncan2018 calls it the half-discriminant, cites Kneser2002 and explicitly identifies Grothendieck’s unpublished Appendix17.16 as using corrected discriminant. These later sources resolve terminology and recognition, but do not date the two discoveries or establish priority between them.',
'Conrad2014,DolgachevDuncan2018','Source comparison only: the later exact citation is evidence of reception of this unpublished appendix, not evidence that its numbering appears in the published EGA IV. Published IV17.16 concerns quasi-sections, a different subject.',group='halfdisc_history')
